                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong Kalb-Ramond field
            or
            %strong B-field
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+U%281%29-gauge+field"} higher U(1)-gauge field
            that generalizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            from point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} strings
          %p
            Its dual incarnation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactifications"} KK-compactifications
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            to 4d is a candidate for the hypothetical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axion"} axion
            field (
            %a{:href => "#SvrcekWitten06"}> Svrcek-Witten 06, p. 15
            ).
          %p
            Recall that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            is modeled as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            in degree 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            and that this mathematical model is fixed by the fact that charged particles that trace out 1-dimensional trajectories couple to the electromagnetic field by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            that sends each trajectory to the holonomy of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            on it.
          %p
            When replacing particles with 1-dimensional trajectories by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} strings
            with 2-dimensional trajectories, one accordingly expects that they may couple to a higher degree background field given by a degree 3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            cocycle.
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            this situation arises and the corresponding background field appears, where it is called the
            %em Kalb-Ramond field
            \.
          %p
            Often it is also simply called the
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              \-field
            , after the standard symbol used for the 2-forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B_i \in \Omega^2(U_i))
            on patches
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cover"} cover
            of spacetime when the differential cocycle is expressed in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cohomology
            realization of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
          %p
            This is the analog of the local 1-forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A_i \in \Omega^1(U_i))
            in a Cech cocycle presentation of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            encoding the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of the Kalb-Ramond field is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} 3-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \in \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            it is given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H|_{U_i} = d B_i
                  \\,.
          %p
            And just as a degree 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
            is equivalently encoded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a degree 3
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
            %li
              %p
                a
